  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\n- Dry weather continues into Saturday\n\nAn upper shortwave trough will pass off to our north tonight and\nhave little affect on our area weather. Upper ridging will move \nin for Friday followed by deep southwest flow Friday night and \nSaturday. High confidence remains regarding the dry weather \nforecast from tonight through Saturday. Vertical profiles of \nmoisture look very dry with mainly clear skies expected. The \nearliest chance for rain will come late Saturday night along the \nLake Michigan shoreline when deeper moisture begins to arrive. \n\n- Cold front brings rain to the area Sunday\n\nA cold front will sweep through the area on Sunday driven by an \nupper shortwave sliding through the Great Lakes region. The right \nentrance region of a cyclonically curved upper jet will be located\nover our area on Sunday. The lift associated with the upper jet \ncombined with forcing via the surface cold front will enough for \nwidespread rainfall. Amounts will not be significant however as \nthe front will be progressive. We are looking at rainfall around a\nquarter of an inch in most areas. The rain will taper off Sunday \nevening as the front pushes east. \n\n- Some uncertainty mid next week regarding precipitation chances\n\nThere may be some light lake effect snow Monday into Tuesday as\ncolder air pours across the Great Lakes. 850mb temperatures drop\nto near -10C. When combined with a lake around +11C delta T's will\nincrease to around 20C. Moisture is extremely limited during this\ntime frame and the flow will be trending north and north-\nnortheast with time. Instability will be excellent, but the \nlimited moisture will keep any lake effect snow in check. At this\npoint expecting little to no accumulation. We will be monitoring \nwind direction and RH forecasts going forward but as of now we are\nnot concerned.\n\nMid next week into the Holiday period there is some uncertainty in\nthe forecast with an upper shortwave trough that moves up the\neastern seaboard. The ECMWF closes the low off producing a\nstronger solution. At this point, the shortwave is well out in the\nPacific. The deterministic GFS and ECMWF have mainly dry weather\nfor us with high pressure in place. The uncertainty comes from\nensemble means of both models that produce precipitation over our\narea. At this point our forecast is leaning dry with only 20 pct\nchances for snow showers in the forecast. We will be monitoring\nthis time frame for trends.\n\n- Mild through Saturday then turning much colder next week\n\nWe will remain mild through Saturday with highs pushing into the\n60s. Given normals in the lower 40s for highs we will be 20+\ndegrees above normal. A reality check will come next week when\nhighs will actually be below normal in the 30s. The cold front\nSunday will plunge much colder air into our region.",
